window.COURSE = {"sections":[{"id":"overview","label":"Start Here","title":"⚽ Gio's Football Brain","group":"Start","groupEmoji":"🏁","emoji":"⚽","blurb":"Your 20-minute plan and the big idea.","kind":"doc","quiz":null,"html":"<p><strong>▶️ Live site: https://roryquarrier.github.io/gio-football-brain/</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Hello Gio!</strong> 👋</p>\n<p>You already watch the football. This course teaches you to <strong>read</strong> it — the way a\nmanager, an analyst and a referee read it. Four ideas that change how every match\nlooks, a short story about Korean football, then a <strong>hard final test</strong>.</p>\n<p>Not easy. You're smart — so this is built for a smart player.</p>\n<div class=\"pic-frame\">\n<figure>\n<img src=\"assets/hero.jpg\" alt=\"A football stadium at dusk with a ball on the centre spot and glowing tactical arrows over the grass\">\n<figcaption>*Same game. Totally different eyes.* ⚽</figcaption>\n</figure>\n</div>\n\n<hr />\n<h2>⏱️ Your 20 minutes</h2>\n<div id=\"paceTimer\"></div>\n\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Time</th>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>The big idea</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0:00 → 3:00</td>\n<td>🚩 <strong>1. Offside, properly</strong></td>\n<td>The rule that almost everybody gets slightly wrong</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3:00 → 6:00</td>\n<td>🧩 <strong>2. Shape &amp; Formations</strong></td>\n<td>Why 4-3-3 is not just three numbers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6:00 → 9:00</td>\n<td>⚡ <strong>3. Pressing</strong></td>\n<td>Winning the ball back — how, where and <em>when</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9:00 → 12:00</td>\n<td>📊 <strong>4. Expected Goals (xG)</strong></td>\n<td>The number that tells you who <em>deserved</em> to win</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12:00 → 14:00</td>\n<td>🇰🇷 <strong>5. Korea's Football Story</strong></td>\n<td>2002, Cha, Park, Son — and what it teaches</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14:00 → 20:00</td>\n<td>🏆 <strong>6. The Final Test</strong></td>\n<td>12 questions, with pictures. Aim for 10+</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>After each step there is a <strong>⚡ Quick check</strong>. Try it <em>before</em> you read the answer\nnotes. Your stars and scores are saved in this browser.</p>\n<h2>🧠 How to use it</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Press <strong>Start</strong> on the timer.</li>\n<li>Read the step. Look at the picture — it is part of the lesson.</li>\n<li>Do the quick check. Wrong answers explain themselves.</li>\n<li>At 14:00, go to <strong>The Final Test</strong>. No scrolling back for answers — manager rules. 😄</li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>🎯 The goal</h2>\n<p>After 20 minutes you can explain offside to your dad, name a formation by looking\nat it, say <em>why</em> a team is pressing, and prove that possession is not the same as\nwinning.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><em>Built for Gio · ⚽ · english, with football words explained as we go.</em></p>"},{"id":"dashboard","label":"My Progress","title":"My Progress","group":"Start","groupEmoji":"🏁","emoji":"📈","blurb":"Your stars and quiz scores - saved in this browser.","kind":"dashboard","quiz":null,"html":""},{"id":"step-01","label":"1. Offside, Properly","title":"🚩 Step 1: Offside, Properly","group":"The 20 Minutes","groupEmoji":"⏱️","emoji":"🚩","blurb":"The rule almost everybody gets slightly wrong.","kind":"doc","quiz":{"title":"Step 1 — Offside, Properly","questions":[{"q":"An attacker's feet are exactly LEVEL with the second-last defender when the pass is played. What is the decision?","options":["The referee must ask VAR","Offside","Onside","Offside, but only if he scores"],"answer":2,"why":"Level counts as onside. He must be nearer the goal line than the second-last opponent to be in an offside position."},{"q":"An attacker's body is behind the defender, but his outstretched arm is ahead of the defender. Offside?","options":["Yes — any part of the body counts","Yes, if the arm touches the ball","Only if the keeper complains","No — arms and hands are not used to judge offside"],"answer":3,"why":"Only the head, body and feet count. Hands and arms are ignored."},{"q":"A throw-in is taken. The receiver is far beyond the last defender. What happens?","options":["Offside, indirect free kick","Play on — there is no offside from a throw-in","The throw-in is retaken","Offside only if he is past the keeper"],"answer":1,"why":"You can't be offside directly from a goal kick, throw-in or corner."},{"q":"An attacker is in an offside position, but he does not touch the ball or affect any defender. The ball goes out of play. Offence?","options":["Yes, but only inside the penalty area","Yes, if he was nearer than 5 metres to a defender","No — being in an offside position is not an offence by itself","Yes — the position is the offence"],"answer":2,"why":"The offence is being INVOLVED in active play, not just standing there."}]},"html":"<p><strong>⏱️ 0:00 → 3:00 (3 minutes)</strong></p>\n<h2>🎯 Goal</h2>\n<p>Say the offside rule <strong>exactly</strong> — the way a referee must apply it.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 The rule in one sentence</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>A player is in an <strong>offside position</strong> if any part of their <strong>head, body or feet</strong></h2>\n<h2>is <strong>closer to the opponents' goal line</strong> than <strong>both</strong> the ball <strong>and</strong> the <strong>second-last opponent</strong>.</h2>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Notice four things most fans forget:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Detail</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>\"Second-last opponent\"</strong></td>\n<td>Usually that is the <em>last defender</em> — because the goalkeeper is the last opponent. But if the keeper runs up the pitch, a <strong>defender can be the \"last\" player and the line moves.</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Level = onside</strong></td>\n<td>If you are <em>exactly level</em> with the second-last defender, you are <strong>onside</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Arms don't count</strong></td>\n<td>Hands and arms are <strong>not</strong> used to judge it. A shoulder is — a hand is not.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Own half = onside</strong></td>\n<td>You can <strong>never</strong> be offside in your own half.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"pic-frame\">\n<figure>\n<img src=\"assets/offside.jpg\" alt=\"Top-down pitch diagram: a yellow line across the pitch at the last defender, one red attacker beyond it, and a dotted pass arrow\">\n<figcaption>*The yellow line is the offside line. Beyond it = offside position.* 🚩</figcaption>\n</figure>\n</div>\n\n<hr />\n<h2>⚠️ Being in an offside position is NOT a foul!</h2>\n<p>Stand there all day — nothing happens. The <strong>offence</strong> only happens when:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>a teammate <strong>plays or touches the ball</strong>, and</li>\n<li>you then become <strong>involved in active play</strong> — you play the ball, <strong>interfere with an opponent</strong>, or <strong>gain an advantage</strong> from the position.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So an attacker can be in an offside position and the referee waves play on, because he\ndidn't touch it and didn't stop the defender seeing it. Referees judge <strong>involvement</strong>.</p>\n<h2>🔒 Three times you can NOT be offside</h2>\n<p>You <strong>cannot</strong> be penalised for offside <strong>directly</strong> from:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a <strong>goal kick</strong></li>\n<li>a <strong>throw-in</strong></li>\n<li>a <strong>corner kick</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>🔑 The sneaky one: <em>deliberate play</em></h2>\n<p>If a defender <strong>deliberately plays</strong> the ball (a real pass, clearance or controlled touch),\nthe attacker is <strong>no longer offside</strong> — a new moment starts. A <strong>deflection</strong> or a\ndesperate block does <strong>not</strong> reset it. The referee must decide: <em>did he <strong>play</strong> it, or just\nget in the way of it?</em></p>\n<p>💡 <strong>Technology:</strong> at big tournaments, semi-automated systems track the players' limbs\nand the ball to help VAR draw the line quickly. But the <em>rule</em> is still the same one you\njust learned.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>🎵 Say this:</h2>\n<h2>Position is not an offence. <strong>Involvement</strong> is.</h2>\n</blockquote>"},{"id":"step-02","label":"2. Shape & Formations","title":"🧩 Step 2: Shape & Formations","group":"The 20 Minutes","groupEmoji":"⏱️","emoji":"🧩","blurb":"Why 4-3-3 is more than three numbers.","kind":"doc","quiz":{"title":"Step 2 — Shape & Formations","questions":[{"q":"Which is the correct description of a 3-5-2?","options":["3 midfielders, 5 forwards, 2 defenders","3 defenders, 5 midfielders, 2 forwards","3 forwards, 5 defenders, 2 midfielders","3 attackers, 5 midfielders, 2 defenders"],"answer":1,"why":"Formations are written from defence to attack, not counting the goalkeeper."},{"q":"A 3-5-2 team loses the ball. The wing-backs drop deep. What shape does it often become?","options":["4-4-2","3-4-3","5-3-2","3-3-4"],"answer":2,"why":"Wing-backs join the three centre-backs, so it becomes a back five."},{"q":"A 'false nine' drops into midfield. What problem does that cause the centre-backs?","options":["He is automatically offside","Nothing — the false nine can't touch the ball","They must swap positions with the goalkeeper","Follow him and leave a hole, or let him go and give him time"],"answer":3,"why":"A false nine pulls defenders out of position or gets free time."},{"q":"Look at the picture in this step. A compact, narrow team is most likely to be beaten:","options":["By headers only","On the wings","By long shots only","Through the middle"],"answer":1,"why":"Narrow = strong in the middle but gives space wide.","img":"assets/formations.jpg","alt":"Eleven red dots in a 4-3-3"}]},"html":"<p><strong>⏱️ 3:00 → 6:00 (3 minutes)</strong></p>\n<h2>🎯 Goal</h2>\n<p>Read a formation <strong>and</strong> know what it is <em>for</em>.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 How to read the numbers</h2>\n<p>A formation lists the outfield players <strong>from defence to attack</strong> (the goalkeeper is\nalways the extra 1):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Formation</th>\n<th>Defence</th>\n<th>Midfield</th>\n<th>Attack</th>\n<th>Personality</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>4-4-2</strong></td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Two flat banks of four. Solid. Easy to defend.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>4-3-3</strong></td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Wide forwards stretch the pitch. Midfield triangle.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3-5-2</strong></td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Wing-backs run the whole side. Strong middle.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>4-2-3-1</strong></td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>2 + 3</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Two holders, one creative 10 behind a lone striker.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"pic-frame\">\n<figure>\n<img src=\"assets/formations.jpg\" alt=\"A pitch tactics board with eleven red dots in a 1-4-3-3 formation joined by thin lines\">\n<figcaption>*A 4-3-3: four defenders, three midfielders, three forwards.* 🧩</figcaption>\n</figure>\n</div>\n\n<hr />\n<h2>🔍 The shape CHANGES when you attack and defend</h2>\n<p>Real teams don't stay in one picture:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Back four → back three.</strong> A full-back \"inverts\" into midfield, so in possession the team looks like a <strong>3-2-5</strong> or similar.</li>\n<li><strong>A 3-5-2 without the ball</strong> often becomes a <strong>5-3-2</strong>: wing-backs drop back to form a <em>back five</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>The \"false nine\":</strong> a striker who drops <em>deep</em> into midfield. Centre-backs must decide — follow him (and leave a hole) or let him go (and he has time).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>🧠 The key tradeoff: width vs. compactness</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>If the team is...</th>\n<th>Good</th>\n<th>Risk</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Wide</strong></td>\n<td>Stretches defenders, opens gaps</td>\n<td>Big spaces between players</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Compact</strong> (narrow &amp; close)</td>\n<td>Hard to pass through</td>\n<td>Can be beaten on the wings</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>🔑 <strong>A manager's job:</strong> pick the shape that uses <em>your</em> best players and attacks the <em>other\nteam's</em> weakness. A formation has <strong>no</strong> magic on its own.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>🎵 Say this:</h2>\n<h2>Numbers describe where players <strong>start</strong>. Tactics describe where they <strong>go</strong>.</h2>\n</blockquote>"},{"id":"step-03","label":"3. Pressing","title":"⚡ Step 3: Pressing","group":"The 20 Minutes","groupEmoji":"⏱️","emoji":"⚡","blurb":"Triggers, counter-press and PPDA.","kind":"doc","quiz":{"title":"Step 3 — Pressing","questions":[{"q":"Team A has a PPDA of 6. Team B has a PPDA of 14. Which team presses harder?","options":["Team B — a bigger number means more pressing","They press equally","Team A — the opponents get fewer passes before an action","You can't tell without the xG"],"answer":2,"why":"PPDA = passes allowed per defensive action. Lower = more intense press."},{"q":"Which is the BEST example of a pressing trigger?","options":["The opponent takes a throw-in near his own goal","The opponent has a clear shot at goal","The referee starts the second half","The opponent passes backwards to his goalkeeper"],"answer":3,"why":"A backward pass gives the receiver limited options — a good moment to close in."},{"q":"What is the main risk of a high press?","options":["Your goalkeeper must leave the pitch","One long pass beats the press and leaves space behind your defence","The referee gives more yellow cards","You can never win a corner"],"answer":1,"why":"The more players push up, the more space you leave behind."},{"q":"What does 'counter-press' mean?","options":["Attacking only on counters","Defending only with 10 players behind the ball","Taking a free kick quickly","Trying to win the ball back immediately after losing it"],"answer":3,"why":"The other team is disorganised right after winning the ball — the best moment to win it back."}]},"html":"<p><strong>⏱️ 6:00 → 9:00 (3 minutes)</strong></p>\n<h2>🎯 Goal</h2>\n<p>Understand pressing as a <strong>plan</strong>, not just \"running a lot\".</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 Three ways to defend</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Style</th>\n<th>Where you defend</th>\n<th>Idea</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>High press</strong></td>\n<td>In the <em>opponent's</em> half</td>\n<td>Win the ball close to their goal → short route to a shot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Mid block</strong></td>\n<td>Around the halfway line</td>\n<td>Stay compact, pounce on mistakes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Low block</strong></td>\n<td>Near your own box</td>\n<td>Give up space, protect the goal, counter-attack</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"pic-frame\">\n<figure>\n<img src=\"assets/press.jpg\" alt=\"Red players swarm a blue defender with the ball near his penalty area while the goalkeeper looks worried\">\n<figcaption>*A press works as a **group**: take the ball-carrier AND the passing lanes.* ⚡</figcaption>\n</figure>\n</div>\n\n<hr />\n<h2>🎯 Pressing triggers</h2>\n<p>A good team doesn't press all the time. It presses on a <strong>trigger</strong> — a signal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a <strong>backward pass</strong> to the goalkeeper or centre-back,</li>\n<li>a <strong>bad touch</strong> (the ball bounces away from the receiver's feet),</li>\n<li>the ball goes <strong>wide</strong> to the touchline (the sideline is an extra \"defender\"),</li>\n<li>the receiver has his <strong>back to the play</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>🔄 Counter-press (gegenpress)</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Lose the ball → <strong>immediately</strong> try to win it back, while the opponent is still\ndisorganised — not run home first.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The idea is the <strong>moments after losing possession</strong> are the best time to attack:\nthe other team's shape is <em>broken</em> and they are close to your goal.</p>\n<h2>📏 A stat for it: PPDA</h2>\n<p><strong>PPDA = Passes Per Defensive Action</strong> — how many passes the opponent is allowed to\nmake <em>before</em> you tackle, intercept or foul. <strong>Lower = more aggressive press.</strong>\n10 means they get ~10 passes; 20 means you're sitting back.</p>\n<h2>⚠️ The cost</h2>\n<p>Pressing <strong>costs energy</strong> and <strong>leaves space behind</strong> the defence. If the press is\nbeaten with one long pass, you're in trouble.\nWhich is why a 90-minute pressing team needs <strong>fitness, courage and good timing</strong>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>🎵 Say this:</h2>\n<h2>A press is a <strong>trap</strong>, not a sprint.</h2>\n</blockquote>"},{"id":"step-04","label":"4. Expected Goals (xG)","title":"📊 Step 4: Expected Goals (xG)","group":"The 20 Minutes","groupEmoji":"⏱️","emoji":"📊","blurb":"One number to judge a chance.","kind":"doc","quiz":{"title":"Step 4 — Expected Goals (xG)","questions":[{"q":"A team has a penalty (xG 0.76) plus shots of 0.05 and 0.12. What is its total xG?","options":["1.93","0.76","0.93","0.17"],"answer":2,"why":"0.76 + 0.05 + 0.12 = 0.93."},{"q":"A team loses 0-1 but had 2.4 xG against 0.4 xG. What is the best explanation?","options":["xG is always wrong","The xG must have included the other team's goals","One game is noisy — they probably were unlucky or the keeper was great","They must be a bad team"],"answer":2,"why":"Over one match luck is large. Over a season xG predicts results better than goals."},{"q":"A team has 70% possession but only 0.3 xG. What does this show?","options":["They were the better team","They must have scored 3 goals","The xG is broken","They had the ball a lot but made very few good chances"],"answer":3,"why":"Possession is not the same as chance quality."},{"q":"A striker scores more than his xG for five seasons in a row. The most sensible conclusion is that he is:","options":["Probably an elite finisher","Extremely lucky every year","A bad shooter","An offside specialist"],"answer":0,"why":"One season could be luck — five seasons suggests real finishing skill."}]},"html":"<p><strong>⏱️ 9:00 → 12:00 (3 minutes)</strong></p>\n<h2>🎯 Goal</h2>\n<p>Use <strong>one number</strong> to judge shots better than the score or possession.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 What is xG?</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>xG = the <strong>probability</strong> that a shot becomes a goal — a number from <strong>0 to 1</strong>.</h2>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is calculated from thousands of similar shots in the past, based on things like\n<strong>distance</strong>, <strong>angle</strong>, <strong>body part</strong> (foot vs head) and the type of chance.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Shot</th>\n<th>Approx. xG</th>\n<th>In words</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Penalty</td>\n<td><strong>~0.76</strong></td>\n<td>about 3 out of 4 go in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tap-in near the goal line</td>\n<td><strong>0.6 – 0.9</strong></td>\n<td>almost a goal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shot from the edge of the box</td>\n<td><strong>~0.05 – 0.10</strong></td>\n<td>1 in 10–20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Long shot from 30 metres</td>\n<td><strong>~0.02</strong></td>\n<td>1 in 50</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"pic-frame\">\n<figure>\n<img src=\"assets/xg.jpg\" alt=\"A penalty area heat-map: red hot zone right in front of goal fading to blue far from goal and at tight angles\">\n<figcaption>*Red = high-quality chance. Blue = low-quality chance.* 📊*</figcaption>\n</figure>\n</div>\n\n<hr />\n<h2>➕ How to use it: add them up</h2>\n<p>Team A takes 3 shots with xG <strong>0.40, 0.10, 0.05</strong> → total <strong>0.55 xG</strong>.\nThat means: on average, those shots <em>should</em> produce about <strong>half a goal</strong>.</p>\n<h2>🧠 Why this beats the final score</h2>\n<p>Football has <strong>few goals</strong>, so luck matters. A team can lose 0-1 while creating\n<strong>2.4 xG against 0.4 xG</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In <strong>one game</strong>, that's usually just bad luck.</li>\n<li>Over <strong>a season</strong> (38 matches), xG is a <strong>better predictor of future results</strong> than goals scored.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>⚠️ xG has limits</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>It says how <strong>good the chance</strong> was, not how good the <strong>finisher</strong> is. A world-class striker may score <em>more</em> than his xG, year after year.</li>\n<li>It doesn't see <strong>the goalkeeper's position</strong> unless it's a more advanced model.</li>\n<li><strong>Possession ≠ winning.</strong> A team can have <strong>70% of the ball</strong> and <strong>0.3 xG</strong> — passing around the box without a real chance.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>🎵 Say this:</h2>\n<h2>Shots aren't equal. <strong>Chance quality</strong> beats chance <strong>quantity</strong>.</h2>\n</blockquote>"},{"id":"step-05","label":"5. Korea's Football Story","title":"🇰🇷 Step 5: Korea's Football Story","group":"The 20 Minutes","groupEmoji":"⏱️","emoji":"🇰🇷","blurb":"2002, Cha, Park, Son - and the lessons inside.","kind":"doc","quiz":{"title":"Step 5 — Korea's Football Story","questions":[{"q":"What was Korea's best finish at the 2002 World Cup?","options":["Semi-final (4th place)","Round of 16","Winner","Quarter-final"],"answer":0,"why":"Co-hosts Korea beat Italy and Spain, then lost to Germany in the semi-final."},{"q":"Which team did Korea beat 2-0 in the 2018 World Cup group stage?","options":["France","Germany","Brazil","Portugal"],"answer":1,"why":"Germany went out in the group stage."},{"q":"Which club did Park Ji-sung play for from 2005 to 2012?","options":["Liverpool","Manchester United","Eintracht Frankfurt","Tottenham"],"answer":1,"why":"Park spent seven years at Manchester United."},{"q":"In 2018, Korea's second goal against Germany went into an empty net. Why was it empty?","options":["The goal had been moved","The goalkeeper was sent off","Korea scored from the halfway line with no goalkeeper","Germany had pushed everyone forward, even the goalkeeper, chasing a goal"],"answer":3,"why":"All-out attack means massive space at the other end."}]},"html":"<p><strong>⏱️ 12:00 → 14:00 (2 minutes)</strong></p>\n<h2>🎯 Goal</h2>\n<p>See the four ideas in <strong>real Korean football</strong> — and know the key moments.</p>\n<hr />\n<div class=\"pic-frame\">\n<figure>\n<img src=\"assets/korea.jpg\" alt=\"A stadium full of fans in red waving scarves and flags, with a boy on his dad's shoulders cheering\">\n<figcaption>*A sea of red.* 🇰🇷</figcaption>\n</figure>\n</div>\n\n<h2>🕰️ The timeline</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Moment</th>\n<th>Why it matters</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>1970s–80s</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Cha Bum-kun</strong> becomes a star in the German <strong>Bundesliga</strong></td>\n<td>Proof that a Korean player could be among the best in Europe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2002</strong></td>\n<td>Korea co-hosts the <strong>World Cup</strong> and reaches the <strong>semi-final</strong> (finishes 4th)</td>\n<td>Beat <strong>Italy</strong> and <strong>Spain</strong> on the way — still the best Asian World Cup run</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2005–2012</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Park Ji-sung</strong> at <strong>Manchester United</strong></td>\n<td>A midfielder famous for <strong>running and pressing</strong> — the work-rate you learned in Step 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2018</strong></td>\n<td>Korea beat <strong>Germany 2-0</strong> in the group stage</td>\n<td>The reigning champions went <strong>out</strong>. Son Heung-min scored in stoppage time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2021–22</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Son Heung-min</strong> shares the Premier League <strong>Golden Boot</strong> — 23 goals, <strong>no penalties</strong></td>\n<td>None from the penalty spot — every goal came from a chance he had to create and finish</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2022</strong></td>\n<td>Korea <strong>beat Portugal 2-1</strong> with a stoppage-time winner by <strong>Hwang Hee-chan</strong></td>\n<td>Qualified for the last 16</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>🧠 Connect it to the course</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Counter-attack from a low block:</strong> In 2018 Korea <strong>defended compactly</strong>, and Germany pushed <strong>everyone</strong> forward — even their goalkeeper. The second goal went into an <strong>empty net</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Offside:</strong> a high line <strong>invites</strong> that long pass — the <em>timing</em> of the run and the line is everything.</li>\n<li><strong>VAR &amp; offside:</strong> Korea's first goal in that match was first flagged <strong>offside</strong> by the assistant, then <strong>given</strong> after a VAR review. Exactly the 'second-last opponent' line from Step 1!</li>\n<li><strong>xG:</strong> one match proves little — but <strong>Son's</strong> consistency over many seasons is what xG people look at.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>🎵 Say this:</h2>\n<h2>A great team isn't one style. It's <strong>the right style at the right moment</strong>.</h2>\n</blockquote>"},{"id":"final-test","label":"6. The Final Test","title":"🏆 The Final Test","group":"The 20 Minutes","groupEmoji":"⏱️","emoji":"🏆","blurb":"12 hard questions, with pictures.","kind":"doc","quiz":{"title":"The Final Test — 12 questions","questions":[{"q":"Look at the pass. Which ringed attacker is OFFSIDE when the ball is played?","options":["The orange ring","Both","The white ring","Neither"],"answer":0,"why":"The orange-ringed attacker is beyond the yellow line (the second-last defender) and ahead of the ball. The white-ringed attacker is behind the line — onside.","img":"assets/quiz-offside.jpg","alt":"Pitch diagram: a yellow line at the defenders, one attacker ringed in orange beyond it, one ringed in white behind it, a dotted pass arrow"},{"q":"Look at the three shots. Which has the HIGHEST xG?","options":["The silver ball — edge of the box","The gold ball — close to goal, central","They all have the same xG","The orange ball — tight angle"],"answer":1,"why":"Distance and angle: close and central is by far the best chance.","img":"assets/quiz-shots.jpg","alt":"Penalty area with a gold shot close in the middle, a silver shot on the edge of the box, an orange shot from a tight angle"},{"q":"The gold, silver and orange shots have xG of 0.45, 0.07 and 0.03. What is the total?","options":["1.55","0.45","0.10","0.55"],"answer":3,"why":"0.45 + 0.07 + 0.03 = 0.55. Roughly half a goal.","img":"assets/quiz-shots.jpg","alt":"The same three shots"},{"q":"This formation is a 3-5-2. When the team defends, what will it most often look like?","options":["A 4-4-2 with no strikers","A back five (5-3-2)","A back three with five forwards","A back two"],"answer":1,"why":"The wing-backs drop back to join the three centre-backs.","img":"assets/quiz-shape.jpg","alt":"Red dots: 1 goalkeeper, 3 defenders, 5 midfielders, 2 strikers"},{"q":"In the same picture, what is the special job of the two widest midfielders?","options":["They stay next to the goalkeeper","They are wing-backs: defend the whole flank and attack the whole flank","They only defend","They only take throw-ins"],"answer":1,"why":"A back three needs wing-backs to give width. They need huge stamina.","img":"assets/quiz-shape.jpg","alt":"Red dots in 3-5-2"},{"q":"Look at the press. Why are the red players standing in the passing lanes instead of just chasing the ball?","options":["To defend a corner","To block the referee's view","Because they are tired","To trap the ball-carrier — no easy pass means an error"],"answer":3,"why":"A press works as a group: one presses the ball, the others cut the lanes.","img":"assets/press.jpg","alt":"Red players cutting off passing lanes around a blue defender with the ball"},{"q":"A goal kick is taken. An attacker is far beyond the last defender and runs onto the ball. Decision?","options":["Goal stands — no offside from a goal kick","Offside","Offside only if the keeper takes it","Retake the goal kick"],"answer":0,"why":"Not offside directly from a goal kick, throw-in or corner."},{"q":"A defender desperately blocks a shot and the ball bounces to an attacker who was in an offside position when the shot was taken. Decision?","options":["Still offside — a block/deflection is not 'deliberate play'","Onside — the defender touched it","No offence because there was a shot","Retake the shot"],"answer":0,"why":"Only a deliberate play (like a controlled pass or clearance) resets offside. A block does not."},{"q":"Team A: 70% possession, 0.4 xG. Team B: 30% possession, 1.6 xG. Which statement is best supported?","options":["The match must have ended 0-0","Team A played much better","Possession and xG measure the same thing","Team B created much better chances"],"answer":3,"why":"xG measures chance quality. B had the better chances with less of the ball."},{"q":"Team X: PPDA of 7.5. Team Y: PPDA of 16. Which statement is correct?","options":["Y presses more intensely","X presses more intensely and wins the ball higher up the pitch","They press the same","PPDA cannot be compared"],"answer":1,"why":"Lower PPDA = fewer passes allowed = more aggressive press."},{"q":"Korea beat Germany 2-0 in 2018, scoring after Germany pushed everyone forward. Which tactical idea does this show?","options":["Playing with no defenders","A false nine","A high press from the first minute","A compact defence and counter-attack — punishing a team that overcommits"],"answer":3,"why":"Germany left huge spaces behind. A compact side that survives can hit them with the second ball."},{"q":"You lead 1-0 against a stronger team with 20 minutes left. Which plan fits best?","options":["Compact mid/low block, win it back and counter-attack quickly","Take a risk by attacking with everyone","Press very high with a high line","Play 7-3-0 and give the ball away on purpose"],"answer":0,"why":"Protect the lead: stay compact, avoid the big spaces behind a high line, and hit on the counter when the ball is won."}]},"html":"<p><strong>⏱️ 14:00 → 20:00 (6 minutes)</strong></p>\n<h2>🎯 The challenge</h2>\n<p><strong>12 questions. Pictures included.</strong> Some need <strong>two ideas at once</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>🥉 <strong>7+</strong> = good manager</li>\n<li>🥈 <strong>10+</strong> = top manager</li>\n<li>🥇 <strong>12 / 12</strong> = a <em>genius</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Rules: <strong>no scrolling back</strong> to the lessons. Look at each picture carefully — the\nanswer is <em>in the picture</em>. If you get one wrong, read the <strong>why</strong> line, then try\n<strong>Try again</strong> and score higher.</p>\n<p>Good luck, gaffer. ⚽</p>"},{"id":"glossary","label":"Football Words","title":"📖 Football Words (Glossary)","group":"Reference","groupEmoji":"🗂️","emoji":"📖","blurb":"Every term in the course.","kind":"doc","quiz":null,"html":"<p><strong>Reference</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Offside position</strong></td>\n<td>Closer to the goal line than the ball and the second-last opponent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Back four / back three</strong></td>\n<td>The number of defenders in a line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Wing-back</strong></td>\n<td>A wide player who defends <em>and</em> attacks the whole side</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>False nine</strong></td>\n<td>A striker who drops deep into midfield</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Inverted full-back</strong></td>\n<td>A full-back who steps into midfield when attacking</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Compact</strong></td>\n<td>Players close together — little space between lines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>High line</strong></td>\n<td>The defenders stand far up the pitch, near halfway</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Low block</strong></td>\n<td>Defending deep, close to your own goal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Press / pressing</strong></td>\n<td>Winning the ball back by closing down quickly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Counter-press (gegenpress)</strong></td>\n<td>Pressing immediately after you lose the ball</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Trigger</strong></td>\n<td>The signal that starts a press</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>PPDA</strong></td>\n<td>Passes Per Defensive Action — lower = more intense press</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>xG</strong></td>\n<td>Expected goals — the chance a shot becomes a goal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Transition</strong></td>\n<td>The moment the ball changes team — attack ↔ defence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Clean sheet</strong></td>\n<td>Not conceding a goal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Stoppage time</strong></td>\n<td>Extra minutes at the end of each half</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"id":"grownups","label":"For Grown-Ups","title":"🧑‍🏫 For Grown-Ups","group":"Reference","groupEmoji":"🗂️","emoji":"🧑‍🏫","blurb":"What this is, and why.","kind":"doc","quiz":null,"html":"<p><strong>Reference</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What this is:</strong> a 20-minute, self-paced mini-course for an ~11-year-old, strong\nreader, football-obsessed student from South Korea. English is the language of\ninstruction; football vocabulary is introduced in context and collected in the glossary.</p>\n<p><strong>Structure:</strong> four idea steps (3 min each) → a short Korea story (2 min) → a 12-question\nfinal test (6 min). Each idea step ends with a quick-check quiz. The test mixes\n<strong>image-based</strong> and <strong>text</strong> questions, and some need two ideas combined.</p>\n<p><strong>Why football analysis?</strong> It uses the child's real interest to practise <strong>logic,\nprobability</strong> (xG), <strong>spatial reasoning</strong> (formations, offside) and <strong>explaining\nreasons</strong> in English — all in a context he already cares about.</p>\n<p><strong>Progress:</strong> saved in the browser (<code>localStorage</code>) on this device only. No accounts,\nno tracking. The student's name and progress can be exported/imported as a file.</p>\n<p><strong>Images:</strong> illustrations were generated with OpenAI image models and contain no real\npeople, logos or text.</p>\n<p><strong>Facts:</strong> historical items (2002 semi-final, 2018 win over Germany, 2022 win over\nPortugal, Son Heung-min's 2021–22 Golden Boot) are well documented; the xG values are\n<strong>approximate averages</strong> and differ slightly between providers.</p>"}]};
